    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight mb-6">Why Plain Water Doesn't Actually Hydrate You</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      I carried a water bottle everywhere for years and still crashed every afternoon. Then I found out why. The answer has been in medical journals since 1960.
    </p>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none space-y-8">

      <p>
        I was so sure I was doing everything right. Water bottle in my bag, water on my nightstand, water before coffee, water after workouts. I was probably the most water-conscious person I knew. And I was still exhausted by 3pm, still cramping on long runs, still waking up foggy after a full night of sleep.
      </p>

      <p>
        I spent a long time blaming sleep, or stress, or my diet. It took me years to find the actual answer. And when I did, I was a little annoyed that nobody had mentioned it to me sooner. Because the science has existed for decades.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mt-12 mb-4">There Is a Protein in Your Small Intestine Called SGLT1</h2>

      <p>
        In 1960, a scientist named Robert Crane discovered something that would quietly change medicine forever. Inside the lining of your small intestine, there are proteins called Sodium-Glucose Linked Transporters. SGLT1 is the one that matters most for hydration.
      </p>

      <p>
        The way it works is surprisingly simple. SGLT1 is like a revolving door that only turns when two specific things show up at the same time. One is sodium. The other is glucose. When both arrive together at the intestinal wall, the transporter opens and carries them both into your bloodstream. Water follows immediately through a process called osmosis.
      </p>

      <p>
        If sodium shows up without glucose, absorption is slow. If glucose shows up without sodium, the door barely moves. But when they arrive together, SGLT1 opens fully and your body absorbs fluids rapidly.
      </p>

      <p>
        Plain water triggers none of this. There is no sodium in it, no glucose, nothing to tell SGLT1 to open. So a lot of what you drink passes through without being fully absorbed. Your kidneys flush a portion of it before it ever reaches your cells.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why you can drink all day and still feel dehydrated. You are not failing at hydration. Your cells are just waiting for the key that plain water does not carry.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mt-12 mb-4">Why This Discovery Matters So Much</h2>

      <p>
        When researchers figured out how SGLT1 actually worked, they applied it to something called Oral Rehydration Therapy. The idea was straightforward: if you mix water with the right ratio of sodium and glucose, the body absorbs it dramatically faster than plain water alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        The results were remarkable. In 1978, The Lancet ran an editorial calling Oral Rehydration Therapy "potentially the most important medical advance of the 20th century." The World Health Organization now distributes it globally to treat severe dehydration from cholera, diarrhea, and other illnesses. It has saved tens of millions of lives.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same mechanism that saves lives in hospitals is working in your body every single day. Every time you sweat, every time you exercise, every time you wake up after a night of breathing out moisture, your cells need sodium and glucose together to actually rehydrate efficiently.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mt-12 mb-4">The Difference Between Glucose and Sugar</h2>

      <p>
        People use the word sugar to mean a lot of different things, so I want to be precise here because it actually matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        Glucose is a single molecule. It is the specific fuel your cells run on. Your brain burns it. Your muscles use it during exercise. And it is exactly what SGLT1 is designed to transport alongside sodium. Glucose is the key.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sucrose is what most people mean when they say sugar. It is table sugar, and it is actually two molecules bonded together: one glucose and one fructose. Your body has to break it apart first before it can use the glucose portion. That takes time and can cause a blood sugar spike.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fructose is processed mostly by your liver. It does not participate in SGLT1 transport the same way glucose does. In large amounts, especially from things like high-fructose corn syrup, it can actually slow absorption and cause digestive distress mid-run. This is why so many people feel bloated or gassy after conventional sports drinks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Real fruit contains both glucose and fructose in the ratios that have existed in human diets for hundreds of thousands of years. The glucose directly activates SGLT1. The sodium from real salt provides the other half of the equation. Together they do what your body was designed to respond to.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the whole reason Purest uses real fruit. Not for the flavor, though the flavor is better. Because real fruit is how you get glucose in the form your body actually recognizes.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mt-12 mb-4">What This Means Practically</h2>

      <p>
        Drinking water is not wrong. But drinking only plain water, especially first thing in the morning when you are already depleted from hours without fluids, means you are asking your body to rehydrate without giving it the tools to do so efficiently.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adding electrolytes, specifically sodium and a small amount of glucose from real fruit, activates SGLT1 and dramatically increases how much water your body actually retains and uses. The afternoon energy crash, the morning brain fog, the mid-run cramps. These are not signs that something is wrong with you. They are signs that your cells have been waiting for the key.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fix does not require a complicated routine. You just need the right things in your water.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl mt-12 mb-4">The Research Behind This</h2>

      <p>
        I want to be transparent about where this information comes from. This is not something I made up to sell a product. These are peer-reviewed papers and institutional findings that have been in the scientific literature for decades.
      </p>

