        <div>
          <p class="font-medium text-foreground">Crane, R.K. (1960). Intestinal absorption of sugars.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">Physiological Reviews, 40(4), 789-825. The original paper describing how SGLT1 cotransports sodium and glucose across the intestinal epithelium. This is where the foundational understanding of the mechanism was established.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="font-medium text-foreground">The Lancet Editorial (1978). Water with sugar and salt.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">The Lancet, 312(8084), 300-301. The editorial that called oral rehydration therapy "potentially the most important medical advance of the 20th century." It is worth reading even now. Available at doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(78)91698-7</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="font-medium text-foreground">World Health Organization (2006). Oral Rehydration Salts: Production of the new ORS.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">WHO/FCH/CAH/06.1. Geneva: WHO Press. The document that codified the sodium-glucose ratios for global use. Available at who.int/publications/i/item/WHO-FCH-CAH-06.1</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="font-medium text-foreground">Wright, E.M., Loo, D.D.F., and Hirayama, B.A. (2011). Biology of human sodium glucose transporters.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">Physiological Reviews, 91(2), 733-794. A comprehensive review of how SGLT1 and SGLT2 work at the molecular level. Available at doi.org/10.1152/physrev.00055.2009</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="font-medium text-foreground">Leiper, J.B. (2015). Fate of ingested fluids: factors affecting gastric emptying and intestinal absorption of beverages in humans.</p>
          <p class="mt-1">Nutrition Reviews, 73(S2), 57-72. Covers how glucose and sodium together enhance intestinal fluid absorption compared to plain water. Available at doi.org/10.1093/nutrit/nuv032</p>
        </div>

        <div>
